Step 4: Ensure local login remains enabled
Check the user's login shell:
getent passwd pdcuser


The output should end with something like:
/home/pdcuser:/bin/bash


If the shell is /usr/sbin/nologin or /bin/false, set a normal shell:
usermod -s /bin/bash pdcuser


Make sure the account has a password:
passwd pdcuser


Do not lock the account password, because the user needs password-based local login.
Step 5: Verify all three requirements
Run these checks as root:
id pdcuser
sudo -l -U pdcuser
sshd -T -C user=pdcuser,host=localhost,addr=127.0.0.1 | grep -i denyusers


Expected results:
Requirement	Expected result
Local login	User can log in at the physical or VM console
No sudo	No administrative privileges are granted
No SSH	SSH authentication for pdcuser is denied

Finally, log out of the root console and test logging in locally as pdcuser. From another machine, test SSH access using ssh pdcuser@SERVER_IP; the connection should be rejected for this user. Other authorized SSH accounts should remain unaffected.
